    @subsection mlalgo_anomaly Anomaly Detection

    Catch fraudulent transactions, equipment failures, and security breaches by automatically
    identifying data points that don't fit normal patterns.

    - @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest" — Detects globally unusual records by measuring
      how easy they are to separate from the rest. A \$50,000 transaction when most are under \$500
      is isolated quickly and scores near 1.0 (highly anomalous). Best for broad screening across
      all data. Uses random partitioning; anomalous points are isolated in fewer splits.
    - @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF" — Detects records that are unusual relative to their local neighborhood.
      A \$200 purchase may be normal for premium customers but anomalous for budget customers; LOF
      catches this by comparing local density. Best when "unusual" depends on context. Uses
      k-nearest neighbors to measure local density deviation.

    @subsection mlalgo_clustering Clustering

    Discover customer segments, group related events, and classify data points — either into
    hard groups or with probability scores reflecting genuine uncertainty.

    - @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN" — Discovers natural groups without requiring you to specify how
      many exist. Groups can have any shape: geographic delivery zones, clusters of related
      support tickets, or network traffic hotspots. Points that don't belong to any group are
      labeled as noise.
    - @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans" — Segments data into a specified number of groups — e.g., divide
      10,000 customers into 5 spending tiers or classify products into 3 price categories. Uses
      k-means++ initialization and supports online (mini-batch) updates so segments evolve as
      new data arrives.
    - @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM" — Assigns each record a probability for each group rather than a hard
      label. A customer might be 70%% "budget shopper" and 30%% "impulse buyer," reflecting
      genuine overlap. Uses Gaussian mixtures (EM algorithm) and supports full, diagonal, and
      spherical covariance types. Supports online learning via incremental EM updates.

    @subsection mlalgo_classification Classification

    Assign records to categories — approve or deny, spam or legitimate, which product
    category, which risk tier. Classification is the most common supervised ML task.

    - @ref Qore::ML::LogisticRegression "LogisticRegression" — Learns a decision boundary to
      separate categories. For example, approve or deny loan applications based on income, credit
      score, and debt ratio. Handles two categories (binary) or many categories (multiclass via
      one-vs-rest). Reports the probability of each category, not just the prediction — so you
      can flag borderline cases (e.g., 52%% approve) for manual review. Supports L2
      regularization to prevent overfitting. Supports online learning via mini-batch
      SGD updates for continuous model refinement on streaming data.
    - @ref Qore::ML::KNN "KNN" — Classifies each record based on its most similar neighbors in
      the training data. For example, if 4 of the 5 most similar past customers churned, predict
      this customer will too. Also works for regression (predict a number as the average of
      neighbors' values). Simple, interpretable, and no training phase — but slower on large
      datasets since it compares against every training record.

    @subsection mlalgo_trees Tree-Based Algorithms

    The enterprise ML workhorses for tabular data — decision trees, random forests, and
    gradient boosting dominate fraud detection, churn prediction, credit scoring, and
    virtually every structured data competition.

    - @ref Qore::ML::DecisionTree "DecisionTree" — Learns a tree of if/then rules that
      split data into groups. For example, "if income > 50K and credit_score > 700, approve
      the loan." Supports Gini impurity, entropy, and MSE criteria. Reports feature
      importances showing which fields matter most. Fast to train and fully interpretable.
    - @ref Qore::ML::RandomForest "RandomForest" — Trains many decision trees on random
      subsets of the data and features, then combines their votes. More accurate than a
      single tree because individual trees' mistakes cancel out. Configurable via n_trees,
      max_features ("sqrt" or "log2"), and bootstrap sampling.
    - @ref Qore::ML::GradientBoostedTrees "GradientBoostedTrees" — Builds trees
      sequentially, each one correcting the previous ensemble's errors. The standard choice
      for tabular data in production (equivalent to XGBoost/LightGBM). Supports binary
      classification (log-loss), regression (MSE), early stopping with validation set, and
      row subsampling for stochastic gradient boosting.

    @subsection mlalgo_svm_nb SVM and Naive Bayes

    Classic statistical classifiers — SVM for maximum-margin linear and non-linear
    classification, Naive Bayes for fast probabilistic classification.

    - @ref Qore::ML::SVM "SVM" — Finds the decision boundary that maximizes the margin
      between classes. For example, separate legitimate from fraudulent transactions by
      finding the widest gap in feature space. Supports linear and RBF (Gaussian) kernels.
      Multiclass via one-vs-one voting with confidence probabilities. Regularization via C
      parameter prevents overfitting.
    - @ref Qore::ML::NaiveBayes "NaiveBayes" — Assumes features are independent given the
      class label, then uses Bayes' theorem to compute class probabilities. Despite the
      "naive" independence assumption, often competitive for text classification, spam
      filtering, and medical diagnosis. Extremely fast to train and supports online learning
      (incremental updates) via Welford's algorithm for streaming variance computation.

    @subsection mlalgo_regression Regression

    Predict prices, estimate delivery times, and forecast demand by learning relationships
    between input features and a target value.

    - @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" — Learns the best straight-line relationship
      between inputs and a target. For example, predict a property's price from square footage,
      number of bedrooms, and neighborhood — the model reports which features matter most
      (coefficients), the baseline (intercept), and prediction accuracy (R-squared). Supports
      online learning via SGD updates for continuous model refinement.

    @subsection mlalgo_timeseries Time Series

    Forecast future values and understand what drives change in data with seasonal patterns,
    such as monthly revenue, daily server load, or weekly inventory levels.

    - @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters" — Forecasts values by learning trend (going up or down)
      and seasonal patterns (e.g., revenue peaks every December, server load spikes Monday
      mornings). Supports additive and multiplicative seasonality with optional damped trends.
    - @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition" — Separates a time series into three
      parts: the underlying growth trend, the repeating seasonal pattern (e.g., holiday shopping
      cycles), and random fluctuations. Helps answer "is this increase due to real growth or just
      a seasonal spike?" Supports additive and multiplicative models.

    @subsection mlalgo_dimreduce Dimensionality Reduction

    Simplify complex data for visualization, noise removal, and faster downstream analysis.

    - @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA" — Compresses many fields into fewer combined values that capture the
      most important patterns. For example, reduce 50 sensor readings to 3 key values that
      explain 95%% of the variance, making it practical to visualize equipment health on a 3D
      plot. Supports fixed component count or automatic selection via variance threshold.

    @subsection mlalgo_preprocess Data Preprocessing

    Clean and prepare data before analysis. Most ML algorithms give poor results when fields
    have very different ranges or when records have missing values — preprocessing fixes both.

    - @ref Qore::ML::StandardScaler "StandardScaler" — Makes fields with different ranges
      directly comparable by adjusting each one so it centers around zero with similar spread.
      For example, if "age" ranges from 18–65 and "income" ranges from 20,000–200,000,
      standardizing them prevents income from dominating the analysis. Use before clustering
      (KMeans, DBSCAN), anomaly detection (IsolationForest, LOF), or dimensionality reduction
      (PCA).
    - @ref Qore::ML::MinMaxScaler "MinMaxScaler" — Converts field values to fit within a
      specific range (default 0.0 to 1.0). For example, temperatures from -20°C to 45°C become
      0.0 to 1.0, making them easy to compare with humidity or wind speed. Useful for heat maps,
      scoring dashboards, or feeding data into pre-trained ONNX models that expect normalized
      input. Supports inverse transformation to recover original values.
    - @ref Qore::ML::Imputer "Imputer" — Automatically fills in missing values so downstream
      analysis can process complete records. Strategies: \c "mean" (average — best for
      temperatures, response times), \c "median" (middle value — better when outliers are present,
      e.g., transaction amounts), \c "most_frequent" (most common value — best for status codes
      or ratings), \c "constant" (a fixed number you choose, e.g., 0 or -1 as a sentinel).
    - @ref Qore::ML::MLPipeline "MLPipeline" — Chains multiple preprocessing steps and an
      analysis algorithm into one unit that you fit and predict with in a single call. For
      example, chain a StandardScaler with KMeans so that new data is always scaled the same
      way before being assigned to a cluster. Prevents inconsistencies between training and
      production.

    @subsection mlalgo_feature_eng Feature Engineering

    Transform raw data into forms suitable for ML algorithms. Encode categorical variables,
    select informative features, and ensure consistency between training and production.

    - @ref Qore::ML::OneHotEncoder "OneHotEncoder" — Converts integer-encoded categorical
      features into binary columns. Each unique value becomes its own column with 0/1 values.
      For example, a "color" column with values 0 (red), 1 (green), 2 (blue) becomes three
      columns: \c color_0, \c color_1, \c color_2. Use \c drop_first to avoid multicollinearity
      in linear models by dropping the first category (the reference level).
    - @ref Qore::ML::LabelEncoder "LabelEncoder" — Maps string or integer category values
      to consecutive integers 0, 1, 2, ..., n_classes-1. For example, ("red", "green", "blue")
      becomes (2, 1, 0) in alphabetical order. Supports inverse transformation back to original
      values. Unknown/null values are encoded as -1.
    - @ref Qore::ML::VarianceThreshold "VarianceThreshold" — Removes features with low
      variance (population variance). Features with variance at or below the threshold are
      removed during transform(). Default threshold 0.0 removes only constant (zero-variance)
      features. Use to eliminate uninformative columns before feeding data into algorithms.
    - @ref Qore::ML::PolynomialFeatures "PolynomialFeatures" — Generates polynomial and
      interaction terms from input features. For example, with degree=2 and features [a, b],
      produces [a, b, a^2, a*b, b^2]. Enables linear models to capture non-linear
      relationships (polynomial regression). Supports \c interaction_only mode (no powers)
      and optional bias column.
    - @ref Qore::ML::SelectKBest "SelectKBest" — Selects the top-k most informative features
      based on a scoring function. Supports ANOVA F-statistic (\c "f_classif") for
      classification tasks and discretized mutual information (\c "mutual_info") for any
      target type. Reports per-feature scores and a boolean support mask.
    - @ref Qore::ML::RFE "RFE" — Recursive Feature Elimination: iteratively fits a decision
      tree, ranks features by importance (Gini/MSE reduction), removes the least important
      feature(s), and repeats until the desired number remain. A wrapper-based method that
      often produces better results than filter methods. Reports per-feature ranking
      (1 = selected, 2+ = elimination round).

    @subsection mlalgo_stats Statistical Functions

    Descriptive statistics, hypothesis testing, and probability distribution functions
    for data exploration and validation.

    <b>Descriptive statistics</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_percentile() "ml_percentile()" — compute any percentile (e.g., median = 50th percentile)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_iqr() "ml_iqr()" — interquartile range (75th - 25th percentile) for outlier detection
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_skewness() "ml_skewness()" — measure distribution asymmetry (0 = symmetric)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_kurtosis() "ml_kurtosis()" — measure tail heaviness (excess kurtosis; 0 = normal)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_correlation_matrix() "ml_correlation_matrix()" — Pearson correlation between all feature pairs
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_covariance_matrix() "ml_covariance_matrix()" — covariance between all feature pairs

    <b>Hypothesis testing</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_t_test() "ml_t_test()" — Welch's two-sample t-test with exact p-values via Cephes incbet (regularized incomplete beta function, same as scipy)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_chi_squared_test() "ml_chi_squared_test()" — chi-squared test of independence for contingency tables

    <b>Normal distribution</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_pdf() "ml_normal_pdf()" — probability density function
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_cdf() "ml_normal_cdf()" — cumulative distribution function
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_normal_ppf() "ml_normal_ppf()" — percent point function (inverse CDF, quantile function)

    @subsection mlalgo_drift Concept Drift Detection

    Monitor deployed models for performance degradation. When the data distribution
    changes (concept drift), a model trained on historical data may give incorrect
    predictions. These detectors tell you when to retrain.

    - @ref Qore::ML::ADWIN "ADWIN" — Maintains an adaptive window of observations and
      detects distribution changes by comparing sub-window means. Automatically drops
      outdated data when drift is detected. O(log N) memory via exponential histogram
      compression. Best for general-purpose monitoring of any numeric metric (prediction
      error, latency, accuracy, revenue).
    - @ref Qore::ML::PageHinkley "PageHinkley" — Detects mean shifts by tracking the
      cumulative deviation from the running mean. When the deviation exceeds a threshold
      (lambda), drift is signaled. Simple and efficient — good for monitoring a single
      metric like average prediction error or transaction amount.
    - @ref Qore::ML::DDM "DDM" — Monitors a classifier's error rate using binomial
      statistics. Provides two levels: \c warning (possible drift, collect more data)
      and \c drift (confirmed, retrain now). Feed 0 for correct predictions, 1 for
      errors. Best for directly monitoring classification model accuracy.

    @subsection mlalgo_evaluation Model Evaluation

    Measure how well your models perform — without objective metrics you can't know whether
    a model is good enough for production or which of two models is better.

    <b>Classification metrics</b> (for models that predict categories):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_accuracy() "ml_accuracy()" — fraction of correct predictions (e.g., 0.95 = 95%% right)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_classification_report() "ml_classification_report()" — detailed breakdown with precision, recall, and F1 per class
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_confusion_matrix() "ml_confusion_matrix()" — shows where the model confuses one class for another

    <b>Regression metrics</b> (for models that predict numbers):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_mse() "ml_mse()" / @ref Qore::ML::ml_rmse() "ml_rmse()" — average squared/root error (lower is better)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_mae() "ml_mae()" — average absolute error, in the same units as your data
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_r2_score() "ml_r2_score()" — how much of the variation your model explains (1.0 = perfect)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_explained_variance() "ml_explained_variance()" — similar to R² but accounts for bias

    <b>Clustering metrics</b> (for models that group data):
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_silhouette_score() "ml_silhouette_score()" — how well-separated the groups are (-1 to 1; above 0.5 is good)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_davies_bouldin_score() "ml_davies_bouldin_score()" — how compact and separated the groups are (lower is better)
    - @ref Qore::ML::ml_calinski_harabasz_score() "ml_calinski_harabasz_score()" — ratio of between-group to within-group variance (higher is better)

    <b>Cross-validation</b>:
    - @ref Qore::ML::CrossValidator "CrossValidator" — splits your data into k folds for
      train/test evaluation, so you can check whether a model generalizes to unseen data

    @subsection mlalgo_onnx ONNX Model Inference

    Run complex models (neural networks, gradient-boosted trees, etc.) trained in Python, R, or
    other frameworks without needing those tools at runtime.

    When compiled with ONNX Runtime support, the module provides:
    - @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel" — Loads and runs pre-trained ONNX models for inference.
      For example, a credit scoring model trained in scikit-learn can be exported to ONNX and
      deployed in a %Qore pipeline for real-time scoring. Supports float, double, int32, and
      int64 tensor types. ONNX Runtime sessions are thread-safe for concurrent inference.

    To check ONNX availability at runtime:
    @code{.py}
hash<Qore::ML::MLCapabilities> caps = Qore::ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (caps.has_onnx) {
    Qore::ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx");
    hash<auto> result = model.run({"input": data});
}
    @endcode

    @section mlpersistence Model Persistence

    Save trained models so they survive restarts and can be shared between systems.

    @subsection mlpersist_inmemory In-Memory Serialization

    Serialize a fitted model to binary and deserialize it back — useful for caching,
    sending over the network, or storing in a database:

    @code{.py}
# Serialize a trained model to binary
binary data = Qore::ML::ml_serialize(trained_model);

# Later, reconstruct it (ready for prediction immediately)
auto restored = Qore::ML::ml_deserialize(data);
hash<auto> result = restored.predict(new_record);
    @endcode

    @subsection mlpersist_file File-Based Persistence

    Save models to disk and load them back:

    @code{.py}
# Save a trained KMeans model
Qore::ML::ml_save_model(km, "/models/customer_segments.qml");

# Load it in another process or after restart
auto km2 = Qore::ML::ml_load_model("/models/customer_segments.qml");
hash<Qore::ML::KMeansResult> result = km2.predict(customer_data);
    @endcode

    The native \c .qml format is a compact binary format with CRC-32 integrity checking.
    All algorithm types are supported.

    @section mlchoosing Choosing the Right Algorithm

    <b>"I need to find bad or unusual records"</b>
    - Do you want to catch records that are unusual across <i>all</i> your data?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest"
    - Do you want to catch records that are unusual <i>compared to similar records</i>?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF"

    <b>"I need to group or segment my data"</b>
    - Do you know how many groups you want?
      - Yes → Use @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans"
      - No → Use @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN"
    - Do records need probability scores for multiple groups (soft assignment)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM"

    <b>"I need to assign records to categories"</b>
    - Is this tabular data (rows and columns) and accuracy matters most?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::GradientBoostedTrees "GradientBoostedTrees" or @ref Qore::ML::RandomForest "RandomForest"
    - Do you need interpretable rules (e.g., for compliance or audit)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::DecisionTree "DecisionTree" — you can inspect the learned rules
    - Do your categories have a clear linear boundary?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::LogisticRegression "LogisticRegression"
    - Do you want to classify based on what similar past records looked like?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::KNN "KNN" (classification mode)
    - Do you need a maximum-margin classifier with kernel support?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::SVM "SVM" with linear or RBF kernel
    - Do you need fast probabilistic classification (e.g., text, spam filtering)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::NaiveBayes "NaiveBayes"

    <b>"I need to predict or forecast values"</b>
    - Is your data a time series with seasonal patterns (daily, weekly, monthly)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters"
    - Do you want to predict a value from input features (price from size, time from distance)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::GradientBoostedTrees "GradientBoostedTrees" (regression mode) for best accuracy,
        or @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" for speed and interpretability

    <b>"I need to understand or simplify my data"</b>
    - Do you want to separate trend, seasonality, and noise in a time series?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition"
    - Do you want to compress many fields into fewer meaningful values?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA"

    <b>"I need to know how good my model is"</b>
    - Predicting categories (fraud/not fraud, customer segment)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ml_classification_report() "ml_classification_report()" for accuracy, precision, recall, F1
    - Predicting numbers (prices, delivery times)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ml_r2_score() "ml_r2_score()" and @ref Qore::ML::ml_rmse() "ml_rmse()" to measure prediction quality
    - Evaluating cluster quality?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ml_silhouette_score() "ml_silhouette_score()" — above 0.5 means good separation
    - Want to test on data the model hasn't seen?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::CrossValidator "CrossValidator" for k-fold cross-validation

    <b>"My data has categorical columns (strings, labels)"</b>
    - Do you need binary indicator columns for each category (for linear models, neural nets)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::OneHotEncoder "OneHotEncoder"
    - Do you need simple integer encoding (for tree-based models)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::LabelEncoder "LabelEncoder"
    - Do you want to remove uninformative (near-constant) columns?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::VarianceThreshold "VarianceThreshold"
    - Do you need polynomial or interaction features for a linear model?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::PolynomialFeatures "PolynomialFeatures"
    - Do you want to select the most informative features automatically?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::SelectKBest "SelectKBest" (filter) or @ref Qore::ML::RFE "RFE" (wrapper, more accurate)

    <b>"I need to test a statistical hypothesis"</b>
    - Are two groups' means significantly different?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ml_t_test() "ml_t_test()" (Welch's two-sample t-test)
    - Are two categorical variables related?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ml_chi_squared_test() "ml_chi_squared_test()"

    <b>"My data has different scales or missing values"</b>
    - Do your fields have very different ranges (e.g., age 18–65 vs income 20,000–200,000)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::StandardScaler "StandardScaler" to put them on the same scale
    - Do you need values in a fixed range like 0 to 1?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::MinMaxScaler "MinMaxScaler"
    - Do you have gaps in your data (missing sensor readings, incomplete records)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::Imputer "Imputer" to fill them in automatically
    - Do you want to preprocess <i>and</i> analyze in one step?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::MLPipeline "MLPipeline" to chain preprocessing with analysis

    <b>"My deployed model might be getting stale"</b>
    - Monitor any numeric metric (error, latency, revenue) for distribution shifts?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::ADWIN "ADWIN" — the most general-purpose drift detector
    - Monitor a single metric for mean shifts?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::PageHinkley "PageHinkley" — simple and efficient
    - Monitor a classifier's error rate directly?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::DDM "DDM" — provides warning and drift levels

    <b>"I have a pre-trained model from Python or R"</b>
    - Export it to ONNX format and use @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel"

